            "actions": {
                "create": [],
                "call": [
                    "NoOp"
                ]
            },
            "readonly": false,
            "desc": "Record the opcode budget available to this call.\nCalled directly it reports what any app call gets; called through an Archon upkeep it reports what a *target* gets, which is the number an integrator actually has to design against.",
            "events": [],
            "recommendations": {}
        },
        {
            "name": "absorb",
            "args": [
                {
                    "type": "uint64",
                    "name": "number"
                },
                {
                    "type": "string",
                    "name": "text"
                }
            ],
            "returns": {
                "type": "uint64"
            },
            "actions": {
                "create": [],
                "call": [
                    "NoOp"
                ]
            },
            "readonly": false,
            "desc": "A hook with arguments of its own \u2014 the shape Archon cannot call.\nArchon stores one blob and sends it as one app arg, and an ARC-4 method with arguments needs the selector and each argument in an app arg of its own. So this method is unreachable through an upkeep today. `scripts/spike_multiarg.py` uses it to measure what a multi-arg call shape would cost, and records both arguments so that a call which loses one is distinguishable from a call that works.",
            "events": [],
            "recommendations": {}
        },
        {
            "name": "report_caller",
            "args": [],
            "returns": {
                "type": "address"
            },
            "actions": {
                "create": [],
                "call": [
                    "NoOp"
                ]
            },
            "readonly": false,
            "desc": "Record who the target sees as its caller.\nDecides whether a target can pay the keeper itself: an Archon-executed call arrives as an inner transaction, and an inner transaction's sender is the app that submitted it. Measured rather than assumed, because a whole class of design depends on it.",
            "events": [],
            "recommendations": {}
        },
        {
            "name": "configure_reentry",
            "args": [
